* {{IPAslink|p}} becomes {{IPAblink|b}} between two i's, e.g., {{lang|nhc|suwapipil}} {{IPA|nhc|suwapibil|}} {{gloss|girls}}.
* {{IPAslink|t}} is dental {{IPAblink|t̪}}. It becomes {{IPAblink|d̪}} between vowels in rapid speech, e.g.,  {{lang|nhc|nitati}} {{IPA|nhc|nid̪at̪i|}} {{gloss|I burn}}.
* {{IPAslink|k}} becomes {{IPAblink|g}} initially, between vowels, and after nasals, e.g., {{lang|nhc|kahli}} {{IPA|nhc|gahli|}} {{gloss|house}}
* {{IPAslink|ts}} is dental {{IPAblink|t̪s̪}}.
* {{IPAslink|s}} is alveolar.
* {{IPAslink|n}} is alveolar but becomes {{IPAblink|ŋ}} word-finally and before {{IPAslink|k}}.
* {{IPAslink|ɲ}} is alveopalatal {{IPAblink|n̠ʲ}}.
* Spanish loanwords include sounds such as {{IPAblink|b}}, {{IPAblink|g}}, {{IPAblink|f}}, {{IPAblink|r}}.{{sfn|García de León G.|1967|p=270}}

